{
  "image_name": "2614e1d1444d7e1c6543ce8d7e3f2ac5.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes a proposed stepwise evolution of the Tibetan–Himalayan region from about 100 to 15 million years ago. Across the series, the landscape changes from separate mountain belts into a broad, high plateau as India underthrusts Asia after collision. Early on, before and just after collision, the region appears to consist mainly of mountains around the Gangdese arc and northern watershed ranges, with lower interior basins rather than a continuous plateau. As time progresses, crust shortens, the Neo-Tethyan oceanic slab breaks off, and magmatic flare-ups and volcanism occur within the Lhasa and Qiangtang terranes.\n\nThe diagrams also show climate and drainage patterns shifting over time: monsoon influence is concentrated on the southern side, while westerlies affect the north, with rivers and internal basins reorganizing as topography rises. By roughly 40–30 Ma, a proto-plateau forms, followed by outward plateau growth toward the Himalaya and northern ranges. In the final stage, a modern-like plateau is established, with high elevations extending across Lhasa, Qiangtang, and parts of Hoh-Xil, while the Himalaya becomes a major southern mountain front. Deep processes such as underthrusting and later lithospheric delamination beneath both India and Asia are depicted as key drivers of uplift.",
